.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt FSMOD 1
.Sh NAME
.Nm fsmod
.Nd FileSystem events Monitor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm fsmod
.Op Fl chfjLv
.Op [-a sec]
.Op [-b dir]
.Op [-p pid]
.Op [-P proc]
.Nm fsmod
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Op Fl F | D
.Op Fl S | T
.Nm fsmod
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Cm ctl status
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -socket Ar path
.Nm fsmod
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Cm ctl pause
.Fl -id Ar owner
.Op Fl -scope Ar all|report
.Op Fl -ttl Ar seconds
.Op Fl -discard-pending
.Op Fl -socket Ar path
.Nm fsmod
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Cm ctl resume
.Fl -id Ar owner
.Op Fl -socket Ar path
.Nm fsmod
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Cm ctl run
.Op Fl -scope Ar all|report
.Op Fl -ttl Ar seconds
.Op Fl -discard-pending
.Op Fl -socket Ar path
.Cm --
.Ar command
.Op Ar arguments ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This utility wait for events happening in a specific filesystem directory, it allows to filter by pid, path and even create a backup of the modified files.
.Pp
With no arguments, start the configured reporting service in the foreground.
The default configuration is /etc/fsmod.conf on Rootful iOS and desktop hosts,
or the matching jailbreak-prefix etc/fsmod.conf on Rootless iOS.
The iOS executable is installed as /usr/sbin/fsmod (Rootful) or
/var/jb/usr/sbin/fsmod (Rootless). No usr/bin/fsmod compatibility entry is installed.
No -C or -F argument is needed in launchd ProgramArguments.
The -D, -F, -S and -T options also use the default configuration when -C is absent.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl C Ar config
load daemon and HTTP-reporting configuration; report_url and at least one
absolute watch_path are required
.It Fl D
run the configured service in the background, returning after initialization
.It Fl F
run the configured service in the foreground for launchd or systemd
.It Fl S
send SIGTERM to the instance identified by the configured locked PID file;
the command returns before the queue finishes draining
.It Fl T
validate configuration without starting monitoring or sending requests
.It Fl a Ar sec
stop monitoring after some seconds
.It Fl b Ar dir
backup directory to store the backup
.It Fl c
follow children of -p pid
.It Fl h
show usage help message
.It Fl j
output in JSON
.It Fl l
.It Fl L
List all the filesystem monitor backends available
.It Fl f
show filename only (no path)
.It Fl p Ar pid
grab events produced by this pid
.It Fl P Ar proc
grab events produced by this process name
.It Fl v
show version
.El
.Sh RUNTIME CONTROL
The configured daemon accepts local control commands without stopping its
backend or launchd/systemd service. Run commands as the daemon's user, which
is root for the iOS packages. The endpoint is a private Unix-domain socket
normally named by appending .ctl to the configured pid_file. With the packaged
iOS configuration, both layouts use /var/run/fsmod.pid.ctl. If that name exceeds
the platform's socket path limit, a deterministic /tmp/fsmod-<uid>-<path-hash>.ctl
path is used instead. The state file always appends .ctl.state to pid_file.
.Pp
Without --socket, ctl loads the default configuration or the -C override to
locate the configured instance. An explicit --socket bypasses configuration
loading. It can therefore control an existing instance even if its saved
configuration has since become invalid. Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/usr/sbin/fsmod ctl status --json
/usr/sbin/fsmod ctl pause --id com.example.package --scope all --ttl 600
/usr/sbin/fsmod ctl resume --id com.example.package
./fsmod -C ./fsmod.conf ctl status --json
./fsmod ctl status --socket /tmp/fsmod.pid.ctl
.Ed
.Pp
Rootless uses /var/jb/usr/sbin/fsmod with the same arguments. The control
status, pause and resume commands return JSON; status accepts --json for
explicit script usage. A zero exit status confirms success. Nonzero means the
operation was not confirmed. The run command preserves child output instead.
.Bl -tag -width "--discard-pending"
.It Cm status
Report ok, pid, monitor_paused, report_paused, queued, in_flight, delivered,
failed, dropped, suppressed_monitor, suppressed_report and holds. Each hold
contains id, scope and remaining_seconds. Counters apply to the current
process lifetime.
.It Cm pause Fl -id Ar owner
Create or replace the named maintenance hold. Owners contain 1 to 127 ASCII
letters, digits, dots, underscores or hyphens. At most 64 owners can hold a
pause simultaneously. Repeating an owner replaces its scope and renews its
expiration rather than creating an additional hold.
.It Fl -scope Ar all|report
The default all scope suppresses both HTTP reporting and log_events output.
The report scope suppresses only HTTP; event logging still follows log_events.
The backend continues reading the event stream in both cases. Events handled
during a hold are discarded for its suppressed outputs, not queued for later
delivery. An all hold takes precedence over report.
.It Fl -ttl Ar seconds
Hold expiration in whole seconds, from 1 through 86400; the default is 600.
Renew before expiration for longer maintenance. Expiration is automatic and
does not require a status query.
.It Fl -discard-pending
Also discard the reporter's preexisting pending queue. Otherwise queued
reports, including an interrupted transfer when capacity permits, remain
eligible after the last reporting hold ends. The queue is only in memory.
Stopping while paused does not send retained reports.
.It Cm resume Fl -id Ar owner
Release only the named hold. Releasing an unknown owner succeeds without
changing other holds. All applicable holds must end before the corresponding
output resumes.
.It Cm run Op Ar options Cm -- Ar command Op Ar arguments ...
Create a unique hold, wait for the pause acknowledgement, execute the command
with its arguments, wait for completion and release only this invocation's
hold. Other owners remain active. The child inherits standard input, output
and error; wrapper diagnostics use standard error without JSON on standard
output. No shell expansion is performed.
.Pp
Foreground commands support interactive terminal input. Shell job control
such as Ctrl-Z, fg and bg is not supported; avoid suspending the command
during a maintenance hold.
.Pp
Options --scope, --ttl, --discard-pending and --socket must precede the required
-- separator. A command is required after it. The default scope is all and TTL
is 600 seconds. The wrapper generates its own owner; --id and --json are not
accepted. All arguments after -- belong to the child, including --socket.
.El
.Pp
For example, pause before an entire package installation begins:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/usr/sbin/fsmod ctl run --scope all --ttl 600 -- dpkg -i yourpackage.deb
/var/jb/usr/sbin/fsmod ctl run --scope all --ttl 600 -- dpkg -i yourpackage.deb
./fsmod -C ./fsmod.conf ctl run --ttl 1800 -- command argument
.Ed
.Pp
The command is not started when pausing fails or the daemon is unavailable.
After a successful pause, run attempts cleanup on normal exit, launch failure
and catchable SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP, forwarding these signals to the child
process group. Its exit status is the child exit code or 128 plus its signal.
An unavailable executable returns 127; other launch failures return 126. A
cleanup error makes an otherwise successful command nonzero, but does not
replace a nonzero child result. Descendants that detach or outlive the direct
command are outside the wrapper's command lifetime.
.Pp
The run TTL is a fixed safety cap and is not automatically renewed. Select a
TTL longer than the expected command duration. If it expires, monitoring and
reporting resume while the command continues. SIGKILL prevents cleanup; the
hold then relies on expiration.
.Pp
A successful pause waits for the event-processing gate and for an active
HTTP transfer to finish or cancel. Begin filesystem maintenance only after
the command exits successfully. A timeout can leave the hold active; query
status before retrying. A request already received by the server cannot be
withdrawn, even if its response is interrupted. Control is not a filesystem
transaction.
.Pp
For devfsev, kernel timestamps suppress delayed events from before a resume
boundary. Backends without those timestamps apply holds at callback processing
time; events delayed within the backend until after resume can still appear.
Unexpired holds are restored before event processing after a daemon restart
on the same boot. A reboot invalidates the previous boot's holds. Preserve the
private state file while a hold is active; events and counters are not persisted.
.Pp
The socket verifies the peer's user ID and opens no network port. Control does
not reload the static configuration. Service lifecycle and control messages
remain logged while event logging is suppressed. In deb maintainer scripts,
pause before changing files and resume afterwards, using the package ID as
owner. Also release on failure paths when possible; the TTL is a fallback.
Use ctl run around the whole package-manager command to cover changes before
a package's maintainer scripts are invoked.
Do not use -S for a maintenance hold because launchd KeepAlive can restart it.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Pp
The configuration uses unquoted key=value lines. Repeat watch_path and
exclude_path for multiple absolute paths. Unknown or duplicate scalar keys
are rejected. See fsmod.conf.example and README.md for all settings.
.Pp
At least one watch_path entry is required; the limit of 64 counts configured
entries, including skipped paths. A watch_path that cannot be resolved with
ENOENT or ENOTDIR produces a warning and is skipped; other path errors remain
fatal. Existing roots are normalized with realpath. Nested watches are allowed;
duplicate canonical watch paths are rejected. Collection uses the outermost
nonoverlapping roots while retaining all logical watch rules. Linux fanotify
keeps every explicit root so nested mount points are also marked.
Each exclude_path must exist at startup.
.Pp
The most specific matching watch_path or exclude_path wins for each path,
regardless of configuration order. Rules match whole path components. A deeper
watch restores monitoring inside a parent exclusion; a deeper exclusion can
exclude part of that watch again. At the same path, exclusion wins. Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
watch_path=/Library
watch_path=/Library/dpkg/info
exclude_path=/Library/dpkg
.Ed
.Pp
This monitors /Library, excludes /Library/dpkg, and restores /Library/dpkg/info
and its descendants. Startup and -T reject a configuration when every effective
watch is still excluded; a restored deeper watch counts as monitored scope.
.Pp
If all watch paths are skipped, the service starts idle with roots=0. The -T
check also succeeds with warnings when the rest of the configuration is valid.
A skipped path is not automatically added if created later; restart to watch it.
.Pp
An optional watch_events selects events for the most recent watch_path, at most
once per path. Comments and other settings do not change that association. Each
new watch_path defaults to all, including nested watches. The most specific
matching watch supplies the event selection; selections are not inherited or
combined with parent selections. Example pairs, commented out:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# watch_path=/private/etc
# watch_events=create,modify
# watch_path=/bin
# watch_events=all
# watch_path=/sbin
# watch_events=create,FSE_CONTENT_MODIFIED
.Ed
.Pp
The case-sensitive groups are create (FSE_CREATE_FILE, FSE_CREATE_DIR), modify
(FSE_CONTENT_MODIFIED, FSE_STAT_CHANGED, FSE_CHOWN, FSE_XATTR_MODIFIED,
FSE_XATTR_REMOVED, FSE_FINDER_INFO_CHANGED), delete (FSE_DELETE), rename
(FSE_RENAME), clone (FSE_CLONE), and exchange (FSE_EXCHANGE). Comma-separated
groups can be mixed with exact event names. The all token must be used alone.
.Pp
Exact names include all types in the groups above, plus FSE_DOCID_CREATED,
FSE_DOCID_CHANGED, FSE_UNMOUNT_PENDING, FSE_OPEN, FSE_CLOSE,
FSE_CLOSE_WRITABLE, and FSE_UNKNOWN. Repeated tokens are harmless; unknown
tokens, empty items, all mixed with other tokens, a missing preceding watch_path,
or a second watch_events for the same path are errors. Rules for skipped paths
are still validated and do not change any preceding effective root's rule.
.Pp
For rename and clone events, each path is resolved independently using the most
specific rule. A final exclusion on either side omits the whole event; a path
outside all rules is not excluded. Otherwise, at least one included path's most
specific watch must allow the event type. Event selection filters
HTTP reporting and log_events, not kernel subscriptions. The all setting only
accepts events the backend provides; it cannot add read tracking or recover the
source of a libc read/write copy. Restart to apply changed rules.
.Pp
Repeat exclude_process to exclude exact, case-sensitive event process names,
for example exclude_process=zsh and exclude_process=sshd on separate lines.
Duplicate names are deduplicated; no process names are excluded by default.
Match the reported data.proc value, not a PID, full executable path, wildcard,
or substring. The rule does not automatically exclude descendant processes.
Kernel collection continues, but matching events are discarded before both
HTTP queueing and log_events output. Restart to apply changed rules.
.Pp
The setting is accepted with every backend. devfsev and fanotify usually provide
originating process names, while fsevapi and inotify may not. A process name may
also be unavailable after process exit or truncated by the OS. Use the actual
data.proc value; events without a process name remain eligible under the other
filters.
.Pp
Repeat watch_uid to accept only selected event UIDs, or exclude_uid to omit
selected event UIDs. Each line accepts one decimal value from 0 to 4294967295,
not a user name, PID, device UUID, comma-separated list, sign or wildcard.
Example rules, disabled unless uncommented:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# watch_uid=0
# watch_uid=501
# exclude_uid=501
.Ed
.Pp
These keys match the backend-supplied data.uid value. With devfsev, the
FSE_ARG_UID/file-info metadata supplies the file owner's UID, not necessarily
the UID of the process performing the operation. Other current backends do not
supply a usable event UID. fsmod does not infer it using its own getuid(), a
later stat(), or process credentials. A zero in JSON alone may also mean
unavailable metadata and does not establish root ownership or a root process.
.Pp
With neither key, UID metadata imposes no restriction. Once any UID rule is
configured, unavailable UIDs are rejected. A nonempty watch_uid list accepts
only listed UIDs. With only exclude_uid, all known UIDs except those listed
remain eligible. exclude_uid takes precedence over watch_uid; the example
above, if all three lines are enabled, accepts only UID 0. Duplicate values
are deduplicated, with no fixed entry count limit apart from available memory.
.Pp
UID rules are global and combine with path, event-type and process rules; an
event must pass every filter. They apply before both HTTP queueing and
log_events output, while kernel collection continues. Restart after changes.
The report JSON schema is unchanged.
.Pp
Repeat watch_gid to accept only selected event GIDs, or exclude_gid to omit
selected event GIDs. Each line accepts one decimal value from 0 to 4294967295.
These keys match the backend-supplied data.gid value. With devfsev, this is the
file owner's group GID from event metadata, not the operation process GID or
its supplementary groups. Current other backends do not supply a usable event
GID.
.Pp
With neither key, GID metadata imposes no restriction. Once any GID rule is
configured, unavailable GIDs are rejected. A nonempty watch_gid list accepts
only listed GIDs. With only exclude_gid, all known GIDs except those listed
remain eligible. exclude_gid takes precedence over watch_gid. Duplicate values
are deduplicated, with no fixed entry count limit apart from available memory.
.Pp
UID and GID rule groups combine with AND semantics and with path, event-type
and process rules; an event must pass every group. They are not paired by line
and do not form an OR across groups. GID rules apply before HTTP queueing and
log_events output, while kernel collection continues. Restart after changes.
The report JSON schema is unchanged.
.Pp
Repeat watch_ppid to accept only selected direct parent process IDs, or
exclude_ppid to omit them. Each line accepts one decimal value from 1 to
2147483647; signs, names, comma-separated lists and wildcards are invalid.
Repeat either key for multiple values. Duplicates are deduplicated, with no
fixed entry count limit apart from available memory. With neither key, PPID
imposes no restriction. A nonempty watch_ppid list is an allowlist;
exclude_ppid takes precedence. Once any PPID rule is configured, unavailable
or nonpositive PPIDs are rejected.
.Pp
These rules match data.ppid: the operation process's direct parent PID
resolved when fsmod consumes the event. Apple devfsev and Linux fanotify
attempt resolution; daemon backends fsevapi and inotify have no PPID
information. This is not a parent name, ancestor, file UID/GID, or stable
process identity. Process exit and PID reuse can affect the result.
For example, watch_ppid=1 selects resolved direct parents with PID 1, not
every descendant of PID 1.
.Pp
PPID rules combine with UID, GID, path, event-type and process rules using
AND: every filter must pass. They apply before HTTP queueing and log_events,
while kernel collection continues. Restart after changes.
.Pp
The default daemon backend is fsevapi on macOS, devfsev on iOS, and inotify on Linux.
On iOS the configured service stays in the foreground under launchd; background fork is not supported.
daemonize defaults to true on desktop and false on iOS. Protect configurations
containing report_token with mode 0600. Desktop HTTP reporting links libcurl
7.32.0 or newer with asynchronous DNS support; no curl executable is required.
A reporter startup error is returned when asynchronous DNS is unavailable.
iOS HTTP reporting uses the system CFNetwork framework. Rootful and Rootless
packages differ in installation paths and require access to /dev/fsevents.
WatchOS and default Android builds provide interactive monitoring without
HTTP reporting. Android reporting requires an explicit target-libcurl build;
see README.md for the cross-compilation options.
.Pp
Events are JSON POST requests. Only HTTP 200 responses succeed; all other
statuses, including 201 and 204, fail and use the configured retries. Response
bodies are discarded, including nonempty bodies on successful responses.
The worker reuses libcurl handles and eligible HTTP connections. The bounded
queue retries failures but has no persistent spool; overflow or shutdown
timeout can discard events. On SIGTERM and SIGINT, shutdown_timeout_seconds is
the queue drain/cancellation target, not an absolute process exit deadline.
At that deadline the reporter cancels the active transfer and discards queued
events. Older libcurl versions can wait for threaded system DNS during cleanup;
resolver or TLS cleanup may extend shutdown. Supported build/runtime libcurl
versions enable best-effort quick cancellation without an exact timing guarantee.
Restart to apply configuration edits. Bearer tokens are
sent from process memory without subprocesses or temporary token files.
.Sh REPORT FORMAT
.Pp
Each POST body is one JSON object with two top-level fields: source is the
fixed string fsmod identifying the reporting application, and data is a nested
JSON object containing all host and event fields, not a JSON-encoded string.
The device/installation identifier is data.uuid. No extra configuration is
required. Receivers must read data.filename, data.uuid, data.type and the other
nested fields instead of their former top-level locations.
.Pp
Configured log_events=true output uses the same envelope. Traditional
interactive -j/-J output keeps its existing format. The HTTP endpoint, POST
method, Content-Type and bearer authentication are unchanged. Only HTTP 200
confirms delivery; other statuses use the existing retry configuration.
.Pp
The following values are illustrative, not values obtained from the current
host; event metadata depends on the backend.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "source": "fsmod",
  "data": {
    "hostname": "my-mac",
    "uuid": "01234567-89AB-CDEF-0123-456789ABCDEF",
    "os_name": "macOS",
    "os_version": "15.0",
    "kernel_version": "24.0.0",
    "backend": "fsevapi",
    "observed_at": "2026-10-26T08:00:00.123Z",
    "type": "FSE_CONTENT_MODIFIED",
    "filename": "/private/tmp/example.txt",
    "newfile": null,
    "proc": null,
    "event": null,
    "pid": 0,
    "ppid": 0,
    "uid": 0,
    "gid": 0,
    "inode": 0,
    "mode": 0,
    "dev_major": 0,
    "dev_minor": 0,
    "kernel_timestamp": 0
  }
}
.Ed
.Pp
The host fields below are JSON strings inside data, or null if unavailable.
Per-event process and file metadata is also inside data.
.Bl -tag -width data.kernel_version
.It data.uuid
Host or OS-installation identifier, separate from numeric data.uid, which
remains the event's UNIX user ID. On macOS, gethostuuid() supplies the host UUID,
formatted in uppercase with hyphens. On Linux, the source is /etc/machine-id,
falling back to /var/lib/dbus/machine-id; it is 32 lowercase hexadecimal
characters. On iOS, the native MobileGestalt API supplies UniqueDeviceID, the
device UDID. This may be 25 or 40 characters and is not necessarily an RFC 4122
UUID. A failed read leaves data.uuid null; no random UUID or hostname is used
as a substitute.
.It data.os_name
iOS on iOS; macOS on macOS. On Linux, NAME from /etc/os-release, falling back to
/usr/lib/os-release; uname's system name may be used when release metadata is
unavailable.
.It data.os_version
The iOS/macOS product version from kern.osproductversion, with ProductVersion in
/System/Library/CoreServices/SystemVersion.plist as a fallback on older systems.
On Linux, VERSION_ID from the same os-release source as data.os_name.
.It data.kernel_version
The kernel release returned by uname. On macOS this is the Darwin release,
separate from the macOS product version in data.os_version.
.El
.Pp
The data.uuid field is a device/installation identifier, not a user ID or
authentication token, and it does not guarantee a globally unique physical
device. Linux
machine IDs can be duplicated by image cloning and can change after an OS
reinstallation or regeneration. Hardware replacement and virtual-machine
cloning can also affect host identity. A hostname change does not make fsmod
generate a new identifier.
.Pp
Host metadata is collected and cached once per process when the first reporter
starts, before monitoring begins. Events reuse that cache without running a
shell command or rereading metadata files. Restart after an OS upgrade or
identity change to refresh it; missing values remain null for that process
lifetime. On iOS, both Rootful and Rootless builds use the system
/usr/lib/libMobileGestalt.dylib without launching a subprocess. The signed
binary requests access to UniqueDeviceID through its MobileGestalt
entitlement; actual access depends on the device and jailbreak. A failed
lookup writes a service-log warning and leaves data.uuid null.
.Pp
For devfsev, data.kernel_timestamp is the raw 64-bit mach_absolute_time() tick
value appended by the kernel as its final FSE_ARG_INT64 argument. It is not
Unix time, seconds, or milliseconds. observed_at is the UTC serialization time.
Backends without a kernel timestamp leave the field zero; fsmod does not
substitute the current clock. data.inode retains the unsigned 64-bit inode
number. Receivers must preserve integer precision when parsing these fields.
.Pp
devfsev reports filesystem changes, not every file read or a complete record
of a program's copy commands. An ordinary byte copy may report only destination
creation, content and metadata changes with data.newfile null. Source reads
do not supply a source-to-destination relationship. fsmod does not infer one
from process IDs, event times or file contents. For a kernel event carrying
two paths, data.filename is the first path and data.newfile is the second;
a rename uses the old and new paths.
.Pp
The backend also subscribes to FSE_CLONE (event type 14). A native clone event
uses data.filename for the source and data.newfile for the destination. This
preserves an explicit kernel copy relationship; ordinary byte-copy operations
still do not provide a source path.
.Pp
A zero data.uid or other zero-valued event field may indicate unavailable
backend metadata, rather than an event from a root process.
.Sh USAGE
.Pp
$ sudo fsmod -a 10 -j | json_pp
